the terminator franchise is the longest  running time travel action film series  and has gotten increasingly convoluted  as its heroes continue to fight the war  against machines if all the alternate  timelines of the terminator franchise  make your head spin here's an  explanation for each ending in the  series so far  in james cameron's action-packed classic  the terminator we meet two men who are  sent back to 1984 to change the fate of  the planet one is an unstoppable machine  with the face and skin of a man that's  arnold's t-800 here to terminate sarah  connor and stop the human rebellion  before it begins the other is kyle reese  a soldier sent by sarah's son john who  has been given the mission of ensuring  her survival until she can give birth to  the future resistance leader john has  given reese a photograph of sarah to  identify her and he's fallen in love  with her before the two even meet  luckily for him sarah reciprocates his  feelings resulting in jon's conception  this means that if john connor had never  instructed cal rhys to go back in time  and protect sarah john never would have  been born in the first place it's a  confusing paradox but it's easy to  overlook considering how well everything  comes together in the film's final  moments in the climax of the terminator  rhys sacrifices his life to save sarah  completing his destiny at least in this  timeline after sarah manages to defeat  the t-800 and escape with her life a  flash forward reveals that she's  pregnant with reese's child sarah  records messages for jon informing him  of his complicated destiny in a scene  that sets up their relationship in the  sequel  if you don't send kyle you could never  be  god a person could go crazy thinking  about this  in the final moments a boy at a gas  station takes a polaroid photo of sarah  the same photo that rhys fell in love  with everything comes full circle  regardless of whether you prefer  terminator or terminator 2 it's hard to  deny that they're among the best action  movies of all time cameron's sequel to  the terminator is a non-stop thrill ride  that proves you don't need convoluted  twists to make a compelling time travel  story and as such it has the most  straightforward ending in the series in  its simplicity judgment day also brings  something to the table the other  terminator movies lack hope sarah and  john connor find themselves safe once  again after arnold's cyberdyne systems  model 101 now programmed to protect john  connor rather than kill him saves them  from the t-1000 in one of the film's  most iconic scenes the model 101 lowers  himself into a vat of molten steel after  explaining that he must destroy any  trace of future technology in the  present his sacrifice fills sarah with  hope for the first time she can imagine  a future where machines and humans live  together in harmony it's too bad that  things would only get worse for the  humans from here  while it's far from the best terminator  movie terminator 3 arguably has the  darkest ending of the series instead of  finishing on a victorious note like  james cameron's original films rise of  the machines ends with skynet finally  achieving self-awareness and global  domination at the end of the mindless  sci-fi romp that is terminator 3 john  connor and his future wife kate arrive  at crystal peak a decommissioned  military outpost where they had been  told they would be able to put an end to  skynet once and for all this was their  destination all along but in a  devastating twist the structure is  revealed to not be skynet's core instead  it is a fallout shelter and the end of  the human race is imminent the machines  won the moment skynet became self-aware  there was never any hope of stopping  them judgment day is here this take on  john's destiny is a subversion of the  expectations of the other movies he was  never meant to stop the war before it  began judgment day was an inevitability  instead he is meant to survive the  apocalypse in order to restart the human  race with kate  terminator salvation is the franchise's  take on a war movie and it's the only  one that doesn't feature schwarzenegger  in the title role starring christian  bale as john connor and the late anton  yelchin as calrisse salvation follows  the pair and their allies as they  attempt a covert mission to shut down  skynet's machine army the ending of this  one is fairly straightforward john  connor nearly gives his life rescuing  kyle reese ensuring his own birth then  former death row inmate marcus revealed  earlier to be part machine sacrifices  himself in the final moments literally  giving his heart to save jon according  to director mick g the original ending  the filmmakers had in mind was much  darker mcgee told io9 connor dies we're  in a room with all the people we care  about you take connor's likeness you put  it on the living machine of marcus he  sits up now looking like christian bale  takes a gun kills kate kills cow kills  star kills everybody eyes flare red the  end  this wasn't the only version of this  ending that was under consideration  according to screenwriter michael ferris  one scrapped ending showed connor's face  on marcus's body but didn't include the  evil turn the writer and director both  seemed to appreciate how ironic it would  be if the leader of the human resistance  was turned into a machine  terminator genesis was an attempt to  reboot the series by doing what the  franchise does best and worst making a  sequel to the terminator what boils down  to an alternate timeline version of  skynet's rise ends up being the most  complicated and convoluted in the series  long history the confusing writing of  genesis certainly applies to its ending  but to explain the ending we have to  start at the beginning after traveling  back to 1984 cal reese experienced his  visions from an alternate childhood he  never lived this splintering of the  timeline leads to rhys taking sarah  connor from 1984 and traveling to 2017  where the upcoming launch of an os named  genesis will lead to the creation of  skynet with the help of pops this  timeline's friendly version of arnold  schwarzenegger's terminator they stop an  evil cyborg john connor and take down  genesis to close the loop the trio  visits the adolescent version of reese  to get him to remember that genesis is  skynet the memory that set the whole  film into motion in the first place to  put it plainly kyle reese needs to tell  the younger version of himself to give  the future version of himself the  warning that causes this timeline to  exist if that can be considered plain  after all these time travel shenanigans  a mid-credits sequence shows it doesn't  really matter though genesis survived  the attack setting up the direct sequels  that were cancelled after the poor  reception of this fifth terminator film  the newest terminator movie is known  more for its controversial and secretly  great opening sequence than its ending  in the film's first moments sarah connor  and a teenage john connor are relaxing  at a beachside bar in guatemala enjoying  some much-needed r r then out of nowhere  a t-800 arrives to kill jon once again  but this time it actually succeeds  although dark fate diverges from the  established timeline it brought back  linda hamilton as sarah connor and rope  james cameron back in as a producer to  create the most well-received movie in  the franchise since t2 still the final  moments are a bit vague with regards to  where the future of the terminator  franchise is heading and whether or not  we'll ever be seeing these characters  again  i won't be back  in the climax of dark fate genetically  enhanced cyber soldier grace sacrifices  herself so future freedom fighter dany  can use her power source to defeat the  evil rev9 terminator this is grace's  fate and it needs to happen so dany can  live and fulfill her destiny in the  film's final scene sarah and danny visit  the childhood version of grace who is  currently 10 years old danny wants to  save her and prevent the events of the  movie from taking place but sarah knows  that they are inevitable dany must  accept that grace has to die to save the  world if not danny will die in her place  and the machines will undoubtedly win  after judgment day a dark fate indeed  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

